## Design, constraints and utility

The R/C/I distinction has concrete value: the supplied `bind_then_read` receipt changes the returned target to b while matching a's objects. A single current-variable map would conflate those observations. Plan lines 29-33 distinguish actual logical membership, expression fallback, graph context, runtime forget and empty-map thaw; hidden aliases are expressly excluded from equality, domain sharing and public names.

The shared engine seams require more than fresh aliases. Current correlation substitutes the bound outer row, Lateral tests compatibility before merging, and Project narrows substitution to projected variables (`binop.rs:264`, `binop.rs:597`, `expr.rs:3309`). Plan lines 54-56 own an explicit correlation mask, shared application policy, logical comparison metadata, projection/deferred/cache dependency coverage and complete visitor/admission/governor/wasm support. Consequently the plan does not assume existing unannotated nodes already implement the oracle law or disguise a second evaluator as lowering. The exact metadata representation remains an implementation choice with an explicit acceptance obligation.

The OPTIONAL table's unit-LeftJoin candidate is governed by the later explicit per-driver policy and stored-once RHS requirement. It is not permission to emit an independently evaluated shared RHS or multiply duplicate drivers. Task 1 must prove retry, context and multiplicity laws before its core unit qualifies. The proportional representation requirement addresses the specialist's concrete subtree-cloning concern without an arbitrary reassignment refusal. These are material design decisions rather than cosmetic enhancements.

`prepare_algebra` admits owned native algebra and `query_prepared_view` uses the shared admission/environment path (`engine.rs:923`, `engine.rs:1034`), matching the proposed one-parser/one-evaluator production route. Plan line 52 forbids applying ordinary logical substitutions after initial bindings are encoded. Task 2 reuses snapshot/configuration/diagnostic/result homes and preserves ordinary Store/prepared/governed/SHACL contracts.

The plan respects the governing Rust-first, one-home, exact-terminal, no new dependency/feature, wasm, determinism and protected-main constraints. The genuine Python oracle/surface tests justify a new narrow Python file with the prescribed explanation, while Rust owns compiler/property/growth checks. It preserves the dirty sibling rather than importing unqualified overlapping work. Required signed hooks run normally; final broad qualification is attributed separately from focused development checks. No licensing copy, global scope relaxation or runtime oracle dependency is planned.

The selected typed compiler structure supplies the utility needed by this issue: composable operator laws and reusable shared row/context mechanics. Declining a broad language framework, second evaluator, runtime dependency and unrelated UPDATE work is a technical boundary, not a cut of a requested reassignment family. No additional enhancement is required to make this plan coherent.

## Completion adversary, pass 1

Applied only the four permitted Stage 1 grounds. No required finding was found:

- UNFALSIFIABLE CRITERION: none. Real inputs are identified by the receipt/historical corpus and boundary matrix; Tasks 1/2 name the executed Rust/shipped Python entry points and exact oracle comparison observations.
- PRE-EMPTIVE DESCOPE: none. All named operators, combinations, repeated/no-init assignments, result forms and preservation obligations remain mandatory. Declined alternatives do not waive capability.
- UNMAPPED REQUIREMENT: none. Every issue acceptance item has a completeness-contract row and extant task with implementation and executable acceptance.
- PLANNED-BY-REFUSAL: none. The plan explicitly requires successful oracle-answering inputs and forbids renamed refusal, new xfails and deleted witnesses. Operational/resource failures remain typed failures rather than reassignment support.

The plan-only mechanical deferral scan found one line: line 54, `deferred substitutions`. This names the evaluator's existing deferred-substitution mechanism and requires complete C/I dependency propagation; it does not defer implementation. No hidden follow-up or scope cut was identified. There is no offending criterion to quote or `REWRITE TO` replacement to prescribe.

