The new paragraph does not assume an inactive compatibility mode costs nothing. It owns the shared enum/visitor/dispatch effects, parser specialization, ordinary layout and allocations, and points to compiled evidence when source alone cannot establish native work preservation. Const-generic admission is a concrete candidate for removing compatibility-only checks/state from ordinary parsing; it is not treated as proof by itself. Boxed/shared policy allocation is restricted to compatibility nodes, while actual native representation and cost still require verification. Allocation and operation invariants supplement the logical argument rather than replacing it with wall-clock observations or an assertion about a usually-false branch. No numeric regression allowance is introduced.

The design requirement is coherent with the repository's Rust-first and maximal-performance constraints and preserves one shared parser/evaluator. First-party allocator/structural-check homes avoid an unnecessary new framework. The final reviewed code must still demonstrate that its representation and generated native paths satisfy these obligations; any extra native cost or missing proof blocks merge under the new row. That future qualification is implementation work, so its current absence is not a Stage 1 plan-only defect.

